## Why This Is Asked
To gauge practical reasoning about logic constraints, Horn-SAT familiarity, and ability to reason about linear-time algorithms and incremental updates in data pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Horn clauses
- Forward chaining
- Partial vs full assignments
- Complexity O(n+m)
- Cycle handling and inconsistency detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function solveHorn(rules, facts){
  const trueSet = new Set(facts);
  let queue = rules.filter(r => r.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)));
  while (queue.length){
    const r = queue.shift();
    if (!trueSet.has(r.consequent)){
      trueSet.add(r.consequent);
      queue = rules.filter(x => x.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)) && !trueSet.has(x.consequent));
    }
  }
  return trueSet;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to non-Horn clauses?
- How would you handle incremental updates when facts change?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason through 3-valued logic and implement a constrained solver with backtracking—a common pattern in policy/rule engines.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic with unknown (U) support
- Implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Backtracking propagation to avoid false outcomes

## Code Example

```javascript
function kleeneImpl(a,b){
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const order = {'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
  const na = not(a);
  const vals = [na, b];
  return vals.reduce((m,x)=> order[x] > order[m] ? x : m, 'F');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize the backtracking algorithm for larger sensor networks?
- Can you extend this approach to handle additional logical operators?
- What strategies would you use to minimize the number of U values in the final assignment?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests translating uncertainty into constraints and performing propagation with Kleene logic.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued semantics; backtracking; constraint propagation; implications.
- Circuit-like formulation; conflict-free search.

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F':x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ const order={F:0,U:1,T:2}; return order[a] < order[b]? a:b; }
function or(a,b){ const order={F:0,U:1,T:2}; return order[a] > order[b]? a:b; }
